```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main()
{
    /*
    ARRAY

    Array is a linear data structure with homogeneous elements/data (same data type)
    that are stored in contiguous memory locations.

    Index starts from 0, and if n is the total number of elements,
    then the index is from 0 to n-1.


    “Array indexing starts from 0 because the index represents the offset from the
    array's base memory address. The first element has an offset of 0, so A[0]
    refers directly to the first element. This also makes memory addressing
    calculation simple: base + index × element_size.”

    Value       Address       Index
    10          1000           0
    20          1004           1
    30          1008           2
    40          1012           3
    50          1016           4

    address = starting_address + (index × size_of_element)

    So for arr[0]:

    1000 + (0 × 4) = 1000

    For arr[2]:

    1000 + (2 × 4) = 1008
    */


    /*
    CREATING AN ARRAY

    Syntax: Data_type array_name [Array_size];

    E.g: int myarray [8];

    When you define the size of the array, all of that space in memory is “reserved”.

    Memory is allocated instantly after the array is created.

    The type of elements and the size of the array are fixed and defined when
    you create it.
    */


    /*
    FINDING AN ELEMENT IN AN ARRAY

    There are three common approaches:

    If the index is known:
    Directly access the element using arr[index] — O(1).

    If the index is unknown:
    Use a searching algorithm:

    Linear Search — works on any array, O(n).

    Binary Search — requires a sorted array, O(log n).

    If we need frequent lookups:
    Use a hash-based structure like unordered_set or unordered_map,
    which provides O(1) average lookup.

    (Direct access, O(1) = constant time)
    */


    /*
    INSERTING ELEMENTS

    Reach to the end of the array and insert the element.

    The insertion at the end of the array takes constant time O(1).

    We can also insert the elements in the middle or the start of the array,
    but existing elements need to be shifted.
    */


    /*
    REMOVING ELEMENTS

    Reach to the index and remove the element.

    Removing an element from the end takes constant time O(1).

    Removing an element from the middle or start requires shifting
    the remaining elements.
    */


    /*
    C++ DATA TYPES

    C++ data types tell the compiler what type of value a variable can store
    and how much memory is required.


    1. Fundamental / Built-in Data Types

    Data Type    Typical Size    Stores

    int          4 bytes         Whole numbers
    float        4 bytes         Decimal numbers
    double       8 bytes         More precise decimals
    char         1 byte          Single character
    bool         1 byte*         true / false
    void         —               No value


    Example:

    int age = 20;
    float price = 10.5f;
    double pi = 3.141592;
    char grade = 'A';
    bool passed = true;


    Type Modifiers

    Type modifiers are keywords that modify fundamental data types:

    short
    long
    signed
    unsigned


    Examples:

    short int x;
    long int y;
    unsigned int count;
    long long int population;


    2. Derived Data Types

    Created from existing data types:

    Array
    Pointer
    Reference
    Function


    int arr[5];       // Array
    int* ptr;         // Pointer
    int& ref = x;     // Reference


    3. User-Defined Data Types

    Defined by the programmer:

    struct
    class
    union
    enum


    struct Student {
        int age;
        char grade;
    };

    enum Color {
        RED, GREEN, BLUE
    };


    Interview one-liner:

    C++ data types are mainly classified into three types:
    fundamental, derived, and user-defined types.

    Type modifiers are not a separate type of data type.
    They modify fundamental data types.

    Sizes are implementation-dependent; the values above are typical on modern systems.
    */


    /*
    STRINGS

    Strings are like a series of characters stored in a specific order.
    Each character in a string is assigned an index, starting from 0.
    */

    string s = "program";
    cout << s[0];


    /*
    FINDING THE LENGTH OF A STRING

    size() and length() are used to find the number of characters in a string.
    Both functions work the same way for strings.

    string s = "Hello";

    s.size();      // 5
    s.length();    // 5

    Time Complexity: O(1)
    */


    /*
    ACCESSING INDIVIDUAL CHARACTERS

    Individual characters in a string can be accessed using square brackets [].
    Strings follow 0-based indexing, just like arrays.

    string s = "Hello";

    s[0];    // 'H'
    */


    /*
    ASSIGNING & PASSING STRINGS

    1. Assigning a String

    string s1 = "Hello";
    string s2 = s1;

    The value of s1 is copied into s2.

    s1 → "Hello"    // Original
    s2 → "Hello"    // Copy

    If s2 changes, s1 does not change.

    s2[0] = 'Y';

    s1 → "Hello"
    s2 → "Yello"


    2. Passing a String to a Function

    void change(string s) {
        s[0] = 'Y';
    }

    string name = "Hello";

    change(name);

    A copy of name is passed to the function.

    name → "Hello"    // Original
    s    → "Hello"    // Copy

    Inside the function:

    s[0] = 'Y';

    s → "Yello"       // Copy changes
    name → "Hello"    // Original does not change

    After the function ends:

    cout << name;     // Hello

    The original remains unchanged because only the copy was changed.
    */


    /*
    STRING COMPARISON

    == (Equality Operator)

    Checks whether two strings have the same value.

    string s1 = "Hello";
    string s2 = "Hello";

    s1 == s2        // true


    != (Inequality Operator)

    Checks whether two strings have different values.

    string s1 = "Hello";
    string s2 = "World";

    s1 != s2        // true


    We can also compare individual characters using their index.

    string s1 = "Hello";
    string s2 = "Help";

    s1[0] == s2[0]        // true  ('H' == 'H')
    s1[3] == s2[3]        // false ('l' != 'p')
    */
}
